questions.description:
A developer adds a counter to a component and is alarmed by what they see in development:

```jsx
let renders = 0;
let setups = 0;
let cleanups = 0;

function Widget() {
  renders++;
  useEffect(() => {
    setups++;
    return () => { cleanups++; };
  }, []);
  return null;
}

createRoot(el).render(
  <StrictMode>
    <Widget />
  </StrictMode>
);
```

This is a **development** build, and `Widget` is mounted exactly once — it is never updated or unmounted afterwards. StrictMode deliberately exercises the component twice to surface impure renders and effects that are not safely re-runnable.

After the initial mount has settled, what are the values of `renders` and `setups`?

Submit them as `CTF{renders_R_setups_S}` — for example, if both were 9 you would submit `CTF{renders_9_setups_9}`.

One click handler queues four state updates:

```jsx
function Counter() {
  const [n, setN] = useState(0);

  function handleClick() {
    setN(n + 1);
    setN((v) => v + 5);
    setN(n + 2);
    setN((v) => v * 3);
  }

  return <button onClick={handleClick}>{n}</button>;
}
```

The button is clicked **once**, starting from `n === 0`. React batches all four updates into a single re-render, then replays the queue in order against the pending value.

The distinction that matters: passing a value (`setN(n + 1)`) *replaces* whatever the queue has accumulated so far, because `n` is a constant captured by this render's closure. Passing a function (`setN(v => ...)`) receives the accumulated value instead.

What number does the button show after that single click?

Submit it as `CTF{count_N}`.

One component, three effects, three different dependency arrays:

```jsx
function Panel() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const [other, setOther] = useState('a');

  useEffect(() => { logNoDeps(); });
  useEffect(() => { logEmpty(); }, []);
  useEffect(() => { logDep(); }, [count]);

  return <span>{count}{other}</span>;
}
```

The component mounts, and then exactly three updates happen, each on its own commit and each actually changing the value it sets:

1. `setCount(1)`
2. `setCount(2)`
3. `setOther('b')`

This is a production build with no StrictMode, so nothing is doubled. How many times has each of the three log functions been called once all of that has settled?

Submit the three counts as `CTF{effects_A_B_C}` — the no-dependency effect, the empty-array effect, and the `[count]` effect, in that order.

Two components set up the same interval in the same way, and differ by one line:

```jsx
function TickerA() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setInterval(() => setCount(count + 1), 1000);
    return () => clearInterval(id);
  }, []);
  return <span>{count}</span>;
}

function TickerB() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setInterval(() => setCount((c) => c + 1), 1000);
    return () => clearInterval(id);
  }, []);
  return <span>{count}</span>;
}
```

Both are mounted side by side, with no StrictMode, and left alone for exactly five interval ticks. The empty dependency array means each effect body runs once, at mount, and the callback passed to `setInterval` keeps whatever variables it captured at that moment for as long as the interval lives.

What number does each component display after those five ticks?

Submit both as `CTF{ticker_A_B}` — TickerA's number, then TickerB's.

A list renders one row per name, keyed by array index. Each row contains an **uncontrolled** input — its text lives in the DOM node, not in React state:

```jsx
function Row({ label }) {
  return (
    <li>
      <span>{label}</span>
      <input defaultValue="" />
    </li>
  );
}

function People() {
  const [items, setItems] = useState(['ada', 'bob', 'cyd']);
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map((it, i) => <Row key={i} label={it} />)}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

A user types `hello` into the input on the **ada** row. Then a new name is prepended:

```jsx
setItems((prev) => ['zoe', ...prev]);
```

The list is now four rows long. React matches old children to new children by key — and every key here shifted its meaning, because the keys are positions rather than identities. The DOM inputs are reused in place; only their `label` props change.

After the update, which row's input still contains the text the user typed?

Submit that row's label as `CTF{label}` — lower case, exactly as it appears in the list.

Two parents render the same memoised child. One passes an inline arrow function as a prop; the other wraps the identical function in `useCallback`:

```jsx
const Child = memo(function Child({ onPick }) {
  log('child ran');
  return null;
});

function ParentA() {
  const [n, setN] = useState(0);
  return <><span>{n}</span><Child onPick={() => {}} /></>;
}

function ParentB() {
  const [n, setN] = useState(0);
  const onPick = useCallback(() => {}, []);
  return <><span>{n}</span><Child onPick={onPick} /></>;
}
```

Each parent is mounted (no StrictMode) and then updated **three** times by setting `n` to a new value. `memo` skips a re-render only when every prop is referentially equal to the previous one, and it compares with `Object.is`.

Counting the initial mount, how many times does `Child` run inside each parent?

Submit both as `CTF{child_A_B}` — the count under ParentA, then the count under ParentB.

Four components, one context, one state update:

```jsx
const Ctx = createContext(null);

function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return (
    <Ctx.Provider value={{ theme: 'dark', count }}>
      <Toolbar />
    </Ctx.Provider>
  );
}

const Toolbar = memo(function Toolbar() {
  return <><ThemedButton /><StaticLabel /></>;
});

function ThemedButton() {
  const v = useContext(Ctx);
  return <b>{v.count}</b>;
}

function StaticLabel() {
  return <i>label</i>;
}
```

After mount, `setCount(1)` is called once. `Toolbar` is memoised and receives no props at all, so its own props comparison always passes — but context updates do not travel by props, and a memo boundary does not stop them from reaching a consumer below it.

Which of the four component functions actually execute as a result of that single update?

Submit their names lower-cased and joined by underscores, in the order they appear from the top of the tree downwards, as `CTF{name_name}`.

A parent and its only child both log on render and in a mount effect:

```jsx
function Child() {
  log('cr');                                  // child render
  useEffect(() => { log('ce'); }, []);        // child effect
  return null;
}

function Parent() {
  log('pr');                                  // parent render
  useEffect(() => { log('pe'); }, []);        // parent effect
  return <Child />;
}
```

`Parent` is mounted once, with no StrictMode. Rendering and effects run in opposite directions: React must render a parent before it knows what children to render, but it will not run a parent's effect until every effect beneath it has run — so that by the time a parent's effect fires, its children's DOM and effects are guaranteed to be ready.

Work out the order of the four log lines on that initial mount.

Submit the four labels in order, joined by underscores, as `CTF{w_x_y_z}` — using the two-letter abbreviations from the comments.

One component declares two effects with the same dependency:

```jsx
function Sync({ x }) {
  useEffect(() => {
    log('as');
    return () => log('ac');
  }, [x]);

  useEffect(() => {
    log('bs');
    return () => log('bc');
  }, [x]);

  return <span>{x}</span>;
}
```

The component mounts with `x === 1` (logging `as` then `bs`), and then `x` changes to `2`. No StrictMode.

A developer assumes each effect is torn down and rebuilt as a unit — cleanup A, setup A, cleanup B, setup B. React does not interleave them like that. Within a single commit it makes two passes over the component's effect list: it destroys everything that needs destroying, and only then creates the replacements. That ordering is what lets one effect's cleanup safely observe state another effect has not yet re-established.

Work out the four log lines produced by the update from `1` to `2`.

Submit them in order, joined by underscores, as `CTF{w_x_y_z}` — using the two-letter labels from the code.

A search box defines its result view inside the parent's body:

```jsx
function Search() {
  const [q, setQ] = useState('');

  function Results() {
    useEffect(() => { mounts++; }, []);
    return <span>{q}</span>;
  }

  return (
    <>
      <input value={q} onChange={(e) => setQ(e.target.value)} />
      <Results />
    </>
  );
}
```

`Search` mounts, and then a user types three characters — one at a time, producing three re-renders of `Search`.

React decides whether to update an existing component or replace it by comparing element *types* with `Object.is`. `Results` is a function expression evaluated afresh on every render of `Search`, so each render produces an element whose type is a different function object than the last. React cannot match them up, so it unmounts the old subtree and mounts a new one — which is why the effect with an empty dependency array fires again, and why any state inside `Results` would be lost on every keystroke.

What is the final value of `mounts`?

Submit it as `CTF{mounts_N}`.
